                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Booster Trooper is an old-school action platform shooter that places speed and freedom of movement above complicated progression systems. Developed and published by DnS Development, it originally arrived in 2010 with multiplayer as one of its main attractions. Its premise is straightforward: players enter compact battlefields, grab powerful weapons, and eliminate opponents while running, jumping, and flying around the environment. Although its presentation clearly shows its age today, the underlying idea remains appealing because aerial mobility gives the familiar arena-shooter formula a noticeably different rhythm.\n\nThe movement system is easily its defining characteristic. Characters are not restricted to fighting along the ground, since flying allows players to quickly change elevation, escape dangerous situations, pursue opponents, and attack from unexpected directions. This makes the arenas feel more three-dimensional than their relatively simple layouts might initially suggest. Learning when to remain grounded and when to take to the air becomes part of surviving each encounter. The freedom can also produce wonderfully chaotic moments when several combatants are simultaneously flying around while exchanging rockets, grenades, and sustained gunfire.\n\nWeapon variety helps support this fast pace. The arsenal includes conventional firearms alongside heavier and more exaggerated equipment capable of filling an area with destructive projectiles. Bullets, rockets, grenades, and magma-based attacks all contribute to battles that favor constant movement. Different weapons naturally encourage different ranges and tactics, so a player carrying a close-range weapon approaches an opponent differently from someone equipped for longer-distance engagements. The mechanics are not especially complicated, but that simplicity makes the game easy to understand and allows the focus to remain on movement, positioning, accuracy, and reaction speed.\n\nThere is a respectable selection of ways to play for a modest indie shooter of its era. Multiplayer is supported alongside single-player content, while Steam achievements, statistics, and leaderboards provide additional goals beyond simply completing matches. The multiplayer focus is where the concept works best because unpredictable human movement makes the aerial combat considerably more exciting. Competitive encounters can rapidly turn into frantic chases as players move between platforms and attempt to gain advantageous firing positions.\n\nThe major problem is that this is now a very old multiplayer game. Its design assumes that finding other people to fight will be an important part of the experience, but players purchasing it today should not expect the population of a modern active shooter. That significantly affects its value because much of the excitement comes from having several opponents occupying the same arena. Single-player functionality at least gives the package some usefulness when multiplayer activity is unavailable, but it cannot completely replace the tension and unpredictability produced by real opponents.\n\nVisually, Booster Trooper is unmistakably a product of the early 2010s indie scene. Environments, character models, effects, and interface elements are functional rather than elaborate. There is little here that would impress anyone searching for modern graphical sophistication. However, the uncomplicated visual style also means that the screen generally remains readable during hectic battles. The game prioritizes clearly identifiable platforms, characters, projectiles, and weapons rather than excessive environmental detail, which suits its arcade-oriented gameplay.\n\nIts simplicity can be viewed as either a strength or weakness. There are no enormous skill trees, elaborate campaigns, seasonal systems, or layers of equipment management competing for attention. You enter a match, move around rapidly, collect or use weapons, and fight. This direct approach makes it suitable for short sessions, particularly with friends who appreciate older arena shooters. On the other hand, anyone expecting extensive progression or a substantial narrative experience will find very little to hold their attention outside the combat itself.\n\nSteam Deck compatibility is a significant concern. The game is currently classified as Unsupported for Steam Deck, with Valve indicating that support is still being worked on. Partial controller support exists, but the Unsupported classification means it should not be purchased specifically as a dependable Steam Deck experience. Technically adventurous users may experiment with compatibility settings, but reliable handheld operation should not be assumed. For this reason, a conventional Windows PC remains the safer environment for playing it.\n\nDespite its limitations, there is still some charm in how focused the game is. Flying around small arenas while exchanging oversized weapons creates a type of uncomplicated arcade action that has become less common as multiplayer games have grown increasingly dependent on progression systems and long-term engagement mechanics. Its combat can be immediately entertaining, especially when enough participants are present to create the chaos the maps were designed around.\n\nBooster Trooper is ultimately easier to appreciate as a surviving example of an earlier generation of independent multiplayer shooters than as a serious competitor to modern arena games. Its mobility system, energetic combat, weapon selection, achievements, statistics, leaderboards, and mixture of single-player and multiplayer functionality give it more substance than its modest appearance suggests. Its aging presentation, limited contemporary player activity, and lack of dependable Steam Deck support prevent it from earning a stronger recommendation, but players interested in inexpensive retro PC multiplayer experiences may still find something entertaining here.\n\nRating: 6/10",
